
  So the fork is: [1mwhat is the durable destination for browser logs?[0m The Node half is the same in all three cases
  (structured JSON, written to a file and/or stdout).

  - [1mOn-device buffer + export[0m — a capped ring buffer in IndexedDB; a support flow or debug command dumps it as a file
    the user sends you. No backend, no new dependency, no data leaving the user's machine without their action. Costs
    you reach: you only ever see logs from users who cooperate, so it's poor for "something is broken for 3% of users
    and nobody filed a ticket." Cheap to add an uploader to later — this is not a dead end.
  - [1mShip to your own ingest endpoint[0m — batched POSTs, retry, offline queue. Gives you the thing you actually asked for:
    logs from production users, automatically. Costs a backend you don't have yet (endpoint, storage, retention, auth,
    abuse protection) and it puts you squarely in data-protection territory, since you'd be continuously collecting user
    activity. This is the expensive, hard-to-reverse choice.
  - [1mThird-party error/log service[0m — Sentry-style SDK. Fastest route to real visibility and someone else runs the
    storage. Costs a vendor dependency, a per-seat/volume bill, and user data going to a third party. Also somewhat
    sticky: the SDK's API tends to spread through your code.

  My recommendation: [1mon-device buffer + export[0m, with the shared logger designed so a network sink is a drop-in addition.
  It gets the logging subsystem — levels, structure, redaction, one API across both halves — landed and useful now, and
  it defers the two decisions that are genuinely expensive and hard to undo (standing up an ingest backend, or
  committing to a vendor) until you know what you're actually chasing. The redaction rule I flagged earlier matters most
  in exactly the cases where logs leave the device, so I'd rather have it proven on the cheap path first.
